# Own-wording Deep Magic spells, chunk A (Abhorrence to Benediction). TEXT = {name: text}.
TEXT = {
"Abhorrence": "You make one creature within range seem unpleasant to everyone around it. The target makes a Wisdom save. On a success nothing happens. On a failure, the next time it makes a Charisma check before the spell ends, it rolls a d6 and subtracts the result from that check. The spell then ends.",
"Abhorrent Apparition": "You carve a terrifying face into a gourd and hurl it to a point within range. Each creature within 15 feet of that point takes 6d8 psychic damage and is frightened of you for 1 minute. A successful Wisdom save halves the damage and negates the fright. A frightened creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the fright on a success.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** The damage increases by 1d8 for each slot level above 4th.",
"Absolute Command": "You take direct control of a construct you built, with a challenge rating of 6 or less. It can handle objects as precisely as its build allows, and you sense through its senses as if you were inside it. It uses your proficiency bonus (modified by its own Strength and Dexterity) and can attempt skill tasks using its Strength and Dexterity modifiers. Your own body stays still as if paralyzed. The construct must stay within 100 feet of you. If it moves farther away, the spell ends at once and your mind returns to your body.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** For each slot level above 4th, you can control a construct with a challenge rating 2 higher, and the distance it may stray increases by 10 feet.",
"Absorbing Field": "You wrap yourself in a field that eats incoming magic and feeds it back into your spell slots. Whenever a spell targets you (this includes multi-target spells but not area spells such as fireball), make an ability check with your spellcasting ability against DC 10 plus the spell's level. On a success the spell has no effect on you and you regain one expended spell slot of that spell's level. If you have none expended at that level, you gain no slot, but the field stays up. If a spell gets past the field anyway, you have advantage on any saving throw it requires.",
"Accelerate": "Choose up to three willing creatures within range, which can include you. Each target's walking speed is doubled for the duration. Each target can also use a bonus action on each of its turns to take the Dash action, and has advantage on Dexterity saving throws.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** You can affect one more creature for each slot level above 3rd.",
"Acid Gate": "You open a portal of churning green acidic vapor in an unoccupied space you can see. It leads to a destination within 100 miles that you know personally and have seen with your own eyes, such as your tower or an inn you stayed at. You and up to three creatures you choose can step through and arrive at the destination, or within 10 feet of it if occupied. If the destination does not exist or cannot be reached, the spell fails and no gate forms. Any other creature that tries to pass through takes 10d6 acid damage and is teleported 1d100 x 10 feet in a random horizontal direction. A successful Intelligence save stops the teleport, but the creature still takes the acid damage when it enters the portal and each time it ends its turn touching it.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** One more creature can use the gate for each slot level above 7th.",
"Acid Rain": "You call down a cylinder of swirling acid 20 feet wide and 30 feet high, centered on a point you can see. The area is heavily obscured. A creature that starts its turn in the area, or enters it for the first time on its turn, takes 6d6 acid damage, or half with a successful Dexterity save. A creature also takes half the damage (as if it had saved) at the start of its first turn after it leaves the area.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** The damage increases by 1d6 for each slot level above 5th.",
"Acumen": "You touch a willing creature. Once before the spell ends, the target can roll a d6 and add the result to one Wisdom check it chooses, either before or after rolling the check. The spell then ends.",
"Adjust Position": "As a bonus action, you shift one willing creature within range 5 feet into a better tactical spot. This movement does not provoke opportunity attacks. The creature moves bodily through the space between (it does not teleport), so a wall or door in the path blocks it.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** You can target one more willing creature for each slot level above 1st.",
"Adjust Positioning": "As a bonus action, you shift one willing creature within range 5 feet into a better tactical spot. This movement does not provoke opportunity attacks. The creature moves bodily through the space between (it does not teleport or gate), so a wall or door in the path blocks it.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** You can target one more willing creature for each slot level above 1st.",
"Afflict Line": "You can cast this spell only as a ritual. You lay a dreadful curse on a victim and the victim's descendants. You need no clear path to the target, only that it is within range. The target must succeed on a Wisdom save or be cursed until the magic is dispelled. While cursed, it has disadvantage on ability checks and saving throws made with the ability you used as your spellcasting ability. The target's firstborn is also cursed, and gets its own save if already alive, or at birth if not yet born. If the firstborn is dead, the curse passes to the next oldest child.\n\n**Ritual Focus.** If you expend your ritual focus, the curse becomes hereditary and passes from firstborn to firstborn down the whole family line, until one of them succeeds on a save and throws it off.",
"Agonizing Mark": "You pick a creature you can see within range as your prey, and a ray of black energy marks it. Until the spell ends, each time you deal damage to the target, it must make a Charisma save. On a failure it falls prone as agony floods its body.",
"Alchemical Form": "You become a body of acidic, poisonous alchemical jelly. While in this form you are immune to acid and poison damage and to the poisoned and stunned conditions. You resist nonmagical fire, piercing, and slashing damage. You cannot speak, cast spells, use items or weapons, or manipulate objects. Your gear melds into your body and returns when the spell ends. You do not need to breathe. Your speed is 20 feet. Your size stays the same, but you can squeeze through spaces as if two size categories smaller. You gain this action: **Melee Weapon Attack.** Your spellcasting ability modifier plus your proficiency bonus to hit, reach 5 feet, one target. Hit: 4d6 acid or poison damage (your choice), and the target must succeed on a Constitution save or be poisoned until the start of your next turn.",
"Ale-dritch Blast": "A jet of ice-cold ale shoots from your hands at a creature or object within range. Make a ranged spell attack. On a hit, the target takes 1d8 cold damage and must succeed on a Constitution save or be poisoned until the end of its next turn. The target has disadvantage on this save if it has drunk alcohol within the last hour. The damage rises to 2d8 at 5th level, 3d8 at 11th level, and 4d8 at 17th level.",
"Allure": "You touch a willing creature. Once before the spell ends, the target can roll a d6 and add the result to one Charisma check it chooses, either before or after rolling the check. The spell then ends.",
"Ally Aegis": "When you see an ally within range in imminent danger, you can use your reaction to shield that creature with magical force. Until the start of your next turn, the ally gains a +5 bonus to AC and is immune to force damage. If the ally must make a saving throw against an enemy's damaging spell, it takes half damage on a failure and none on a success. The shield gives no protection against psychic damage from any source.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** You can target one more ally for each slot level above 6th.",
"Alone": "You make a creature within range believe its allies have been banished to another realm. The target must succeed on a Wisdom save, or it treats its allies as if they were invisible and silenced. It cannot target, perceive, or otherwise interact with them for the duration. If one of its allies hits it with a melee attack, the creature can make another Wisdom save, ending the spell on a success.",
"Alter Arrow's Fortune": "You clap your hands and set off tiny events that spoil an enemy's aim. When an enemy makes a ranged attack, with a weapon or a spell, that hits one of your allies, the enemy must reroll the attack unless it succeeds on a Charisma save. The attack uses the lower of the two rolls, which effectively gives the enemy disadvantage.",
"Alter Time Flow": "You bend the flow of time for every creature within 30 feet of you. You can give each creature either a haste or a slow effect (as the spells). An unwilling creature makes a Constitution save and avoids the effect on a success. Each unwilling creature that stays in the area must make a new save at the end of its turn. An affected creature returns to normal when it leaves the area, but if it re-enters while the spell lasts, it is affected again and gets another save if unwilling.",
"Althea's Travel Tent": "You touch an ordinary, properly pitched canvas tent to make a comfortable place for you and one companion to sleep. It looks normal outside, but inside it has a small foyer with a writing desk and chair, and a larger bedchamber with a soft bed for two, a nightstand with a candle, and a clothes rack. The floors are clean, dry, hard-packed ground. When the spell ends, the tent and ground return to normal and any creatures inside are expelled to the nearest open spaces.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** With a 3rd-level slot, the foyer becomes a dining area seating six, with floor space for six to sleep if they bring bedding. With a 4th-level slot, the temperature inside is comfortable whatever the weather and the dining area gains a small kitchen. With a 5th-level slot, an unseen servant prepares and serves food from your supplies. With a 6th-level slot, a third room is added with three two-person beds. With a slot of 7th level or higher, the dining area and second sleeping area can each hold eight people.",
"Amplify Gravity": "You intensify gravity in a 50-foot radius within range. Inside the area, falling damage is quadrupled (2d6 per 5 feet fallen) with a maximum of 40d6. Any creature on the ground in the area when you cast the spell must succeed on a Strength save or be knocked prone. The same applies to a creature that enters the area or ends its turn there. A prone creature in the area must succeed on a Strength save to stand up. A creature on the ground in the area moves at half speed and has disadvantage on Dexterity checks and ranged attack rolls.",
"Amplify Ley Field": "You create a faintly shimmering field of charged energy around yourself. Inside it, any ley line you can draw on becomes stronger: weak lines become strong and strong lines become titanic. If no ley line is near enough to draw on, you can treat the spell's area itself as an unlocked, weak ley line.",
"Amplify Light": "You touch a nonmagical light source and make it brighter. While the spell lasts, the radius of bright light is doubled, and dim light extends the same distance beyond that. The source burns for half as long: a torch lasts half an hour and a hooded lantern burns its fuel in 3 hours. Adding fuel to the source ends the spell and returns it to normal brightness.",
"Analyze Device": "You learn every mechanical property, mechanism, and function of one construct or clockwork device, including how to activate or deactivate those functions where that applies.",
"Ancestor's Strength": "Choose a willing creature you can see and touch. Its muscles swell with vigor. For the duration, the target counts as one size larger for carrying capacity, the most it can lift, push, or pull, and its ability to break objects. It also has advantage on Strength checks.",
"Anchoring Rope": "You create a spectral lanyard. One end ties around your waist and the other is anchored in midair at a point you pick within range. The rope can be 5 to 30 feet long and holds up to 800 pounds. The anchor point cannot be moved after casting. If you cast the spell as a reaction while falling, you stop at a point of your choice in midair and take no falling damage. You can dismiss the rope as a bonus action.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** For every two slot levels above 1st, you can create one more rope, and each rope must be attached to a different creature.",
"Ancient Shade": "You give a pile of bones, or even bone dust, within range a trace of life and intellect so its ancient spirit can answer your questions. The remains can be of undead, including mindless undead such as skeletons and zombies, but intelligent undead are not affected. The older the spirit, the less it remembers. You can ask up to five questions if it died within the past year, four within ten years, three within one hundred years, two within one thousand years, and one if it died more than one thousand years ago. The shade knows only what it knew in life, including languages. Answers are usually brief, cryptic, or repetitive, and it need not answer truthfully if you are hostile or it sees you as an enemy. The spell returns only the animating spirit, not the soul, so the shade cannot learn anything new, does not understand events since its death, and cannot guess at the future.",
"Angelic Guardian": "You conjure a minor celestial image to guard a creature you can see within range. A faintly glowing head and shoulders hover within 5 feet of the target and move to block incoming attacks, giving the target a +2 bonus to AC. The first time the target fails a Dexterity save while the spell is active, it can use its reaction to reroll the save, and the spell then ends.",
"Animal Spy": "You create a mental link between you and a beast within range. For the duration, you can use a bonus action to move your awareness into the beast, using its sight, hearing, smell, taste, and touch, and another bonus action on a later turn to return to your body. You can use an action to end the spell. It works on normal beasts, including giant versions, but not conjured animals or familiars. You do not control the beast or make it friendly. While you use its senses, your body is motionless and effectively unconscious. The spell ends if the beast gets more than 1 mile from you or is killed. If you are using its senses when it is killed, you must succeed on a DC 14 Wisdom save or be stunned for 1d4 rounds.",
"Animate Construct": "You animate a carefully prepared Tiny construct. It acts immediately on your turn and attacks your enemies as best it can. You decide whether it attacks, whom it targets, or what else it does, and you can pick a different object each time you cast the spell. The body costs 10 gp times its hit points and can be reused if it is not badly damaged or destroyed. If no prepared body is available, you can animate loose metal or stone instead. You must first arrange it into a suitable shape (up to a minute), and the construct's hit points are halved. The construct has Constitution 10, Intelligence 3, Wisdom 3, and Charisma 1. Other statistics depend on size. Tiny: 15 hp, AC 12, 3 to hit, 1d4 + 4 damage. Small: 25 hp, AC 13, 4 to hit, 1d8 + 2 damage (slot level 2). Medium: 40 hp, AC 14, 5 to hit, 2d6 + 1 damage (slot level 3). Large: 50 hp, AC 15, 6 to hit, 2d10 + 2 damage (slot level 4). Huge: 80 hp, AC 16, 8 to hit, 2d12 + 4 damage (slot level 5). Gargantuan: 100 hp, AC 17, 10 to hit, 4d8 + 6 damage (slot level 6).\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Casting with a higher slot lets you animate a larger construct, as listed above.",
"Animate Ghoul": "You raise one Medium or Small humanoid corpse as a ghoul under your control. Any class levels or abilities it had in life are lost and replaced by the standard ghoul stat block.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** With a 3rd-level slot, you can use the corpse of a Large humanoid to create a Large ghoul. With a slot of 4th level or higher, the spell creates a ghast instead, and the material component becomes an onyx gemstone worth at least 200 gp.",
"Animate Greater Undead": "From a pile of bones or the corpse of a Large or Huge humanoid within range, you raise an undead servant, either a skeleton or a zombie. A skeleton uses the stat block of a minotaur skeleton and a zombie uses that of an ogre zombie, unless a more fitting stat block is available. The creature is under your control for 24 hours, after which it stops obeying you. To keep control for another 24 hours, you must cast the spell on it again while you still control it. Casting it this way reasserts control over up to four creatures you have already animated, instead of animating a new one.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** You can reanimate one more creature for each slot level above 6th.",
"Animated Object Swarm": "Countless Tiny objects come alive at your command and form a swarm. They follow the rules of the animate objects spell, except you animate only Tiny objects, which gather into a Medium swarm. The swarm's area is difficult terrain, and creatures in it are lightly obscured. The source refers to a separate swarm stat block that is not included in the spell text.",
"Animated Scroll": "Fold paper or parchment into the shape of an animal before casting. It becomes a paper animal of the kind it most resembles, using the stat block of any beast with a challenge rating of 0. It is made of paper but can do anything the real animal can do. A paper owl can fly and strike with talons, and a paper frog can swim without falling apart. It follows your commands as best it can, including carrying messages to a recipient whose location you know. You can have only one paper animal at a time. If you cast the spell while you already have one, you instead make it change into a different beast of challenge rating 0 that you choose. The duration is 24 hours, increasing to 48 hours at 5th level, 72 hours at 11th level, and 96 hours at 17th level.",
"Anomalous Object": "By touching an object, you pull a second version of it from elsewhere in time. If the object is held or controlled by a creature, you must hit that creature with a melee spell attack. Any effect on one copy also affects the other, such as spent charges or damage taken. If either copy is destroyed, both are destroyed. The spell does not work on sentient items or unique artifacts.",
"Anticipate Arcana": "Foresight gives you a moment to brace against magic. When you cast this spell as a reaction to an enemy you can see casting a spell, you have advantage on saving throws against spells and other magical effects until the start of your next turn.",
"Anticipate Attack": "In a flash of foreknowledge, you see an attack coming. As a reaction when you are attacked, before the attack roll is made, you can cast this spell and move up to half your speed without provoking opportunity attacks. The attack still happens, but it automatically misses if you are no longer within its range, are in a space it cannot hit, or can no longer be targeted by it. If none of that applies but the situation has changed, for example you now have cover, the attack is made under the new conditions.",
"Anticipate Weakness": "With a quick glimpse of the future, you spot the gap about to open in a foe's defense and strike. After you cast this spell as a bonus action, you have advantage on all attack rolls until the end of your turn.",
"Arcane Parasite": "You link yourself to a nearby source of arcane power and draw on it to fuel your casting. When you cast a spell, you can use a bonus action to draw on the link, and you then spend no spell slot for that spell because the energy comes from the outside source. Each spell cast this way shortens this spell's remaining duration by 10 minutes per level of the spell cast. For example, casting fireball through the link costs no slot but reduces the duration by 30 minutes. If the remaining duration is equal to or less than the spell's level times 10 minutes, this spell ends as soon as that spell is cast.",
"Arcane Sight": "The recipient gains the benefits of both true seeing and detect magic until the spell ends. The recipient also learns the name and effect of every spell it witnesses during the duration.",
"Armored Heart": "The target gains resistance to bludgeoning, piercing, and slashing damage. Adamantine or magical weapons overcome this resistance.",
"Armored Shell": "You create a suit of magical studded leather armor (AC 12). It does not give you proficiency with armor. If you lack the right armor proficiency, you have disadvantage on any ability check, saving throw, or attack roll that uses Strength or Dexterity, and you cannot cast spells.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** A higher slot creates stronger armor: a chain shirt (AC 13) at 2nd level, scale mail (AC 14) at 3rd level, chain mail (AC 16) at 4th level, and plate armor (AC 18) at 5th level.",
"As You Were": "Cast on a dead or undead body, this spell restores the creature to how it looked in life when healthy and uninjured. The target must have a physical body, so the spell fails on a noncorporeal one. On a corpse it works like gentle repose, except the corpse also looks healthy and uninjured. An undead target is likewise restored to its living look, even if it died of disease, wounds, or centuries ago. The target looks, smells, and sounds (if it can speak) as it did in life. Friends and family notice something wrong only with a successful Wisdom (Insight) check against your spell save DC, and only if they have reason to be suspicious, such as knowing the person should be dead. Spells and abilities that detect undead are also fooled, but the creature can still be affected by Turn Undead as normal. The spell does not give speech to undead that cannot speak. The creature eats, drinks, and breathes like a living one and can mimic sleep without needing it. Any radiant or necrotic damage, or any effect that reduces its Constitution, ends the spell. If the target is an undead creature that is not your ally or under your control, it makes a Charisma save to resist.",
"Ashen Memories": "You touch ashes, embers, or soot left by a fire and receive a vision of one significant event that happened nearby while the fire burned. Touching the cold embers of a campfire might show a snippet of conversation held around it, and touching the ashes of a burned letter might show who destroyed it or what it said. You do not control what you see. The vision usually relates to the most meaningful event tied to that fire, and the GM decides the details.",
"Aspect of the Ape": "You give a creature you touch a more apelike form. For the duration, the target gains a climbing speed equal to its walking speed and advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks made to jump or climb. While in tree branches or a similar area (at the GM's discretion), the target can move up to its climbing speed as a bonus action by swinging to a nearby tree if the branches are close enough. This bonus action requires two free hands, or one free hand if the target is barefoot.",
"Aspect of the Dragon": "You draw out the ancient nature in your blood and take the form of any dragon-type creature of challenge rating 10 or less. You assume the new form's hit points and Hit Dice. When you revert, you return to the hit points you had before. If you revert by dropping to 0 hit points, excess damage carries over to your normal form, and if that does not reduce it to 0 you are not knocked unconscious. You keep the benefits of your class, race, and other features and can use them if the new form is physically able. You can speak only if the dragon normally can. When you transform, you choose whether your equipment falls to the ground, merges into the form, or is worn by it. Worn equipment works normally but does not change shape or size, and equipment the form cannot wear must fall or merge. The GM decides whether the form can wear or use a given item. Merged equipment has no effect.",
"Aspect of the Firebird": "You take on the aspect of a firebird, including some of its physical traits. A fiery mantle shaped like feathery wings and a broad tail settles on you. You gain immunity to fire and a flying speed of 60 feet. Creatures within 5 feet of you gain immunity to natural, environmental cold. As an action, you can loose a blast of light 5 feet wide and 50 feet long. Each creature in its path takes 6d6 fire damage and is blinded for 1d4 rounds, or takes half damage and is not blinded with a successful Dexterity save. Each use of this blast reduces the spell's duration by 1 hour. When you spend the last hour, the spell ends at the start of your next turn.",
"Aspect of the Ram": "You take on the aspect of a ram. Your body hair grows thick and woolly and a pair of curling horns sprouts from your head. As an action you can make a ram attack with the horns, and you are proficient with it. It deals 1d6 bludgeoning damage on a hit. If you move at least 20 feet straight toward a target and hit with the attack on the same turn, the target takes an extra 1d6 bludgeoning damage, and if it is a creature it must succeed on a Strength check against your spell save DC or be knocked prone. You ignore difficult terrain from rubble, ice sheets, scree, or steep slopes. You have advantage on Strength (Athletics) checks to climb or jump, and on Dexterity (Acrobatics) checks to balance or stay on your feet.",
"Aspect of the Serpent": "A creature you touch takes on snakelike traits. Its tongue grows long and forked, its canines become venomous fangs, and its pupils turn to vertical slits. The target gains darkvision out to 60 feet and blindsight out to 30 feet. As a bonus action when you cast the spell, the target can make a ranged weapon attack with a normal range of 60 feet that deals 2d6 poison damage on a hit. As an action, the target can make a bite attack using Strength or Dexterity (melee, reach 5 feet, one creature). On a hit it deals 2d6 piercing damage and the creature must succeed on a DC 14 Constitution save or be paralyzed for 1 minute. A paralyzed creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, ending the paralysis on a success.",
"Aura of Entropy": "Sickening energy spreads from you in a sphere with a 30-foot radius. For the duration, it stays centered on you and moves with you. Each hostile creature in the sphere has disadvantage on saves against becoming frightened, paralyzed, poisoned, or stunned. Creatures in the sphere cannot gain temporary hit points, and any effect that restores hit points to a creature in it restores that many fewer hit points, reduced by your spellcasting ability modifier (minimum 1).",
"Aura of Listlessness": "You create an aura that saps the will of creatures and leaves them unable to act. Any creature within 20 feet of you must succeed on a Wisdom save or be unable to take actions, reactions, or bonus actions on its turn, except the Disengage action. Affected creatures can still move, but only to get away from harm, such as attacks from other creatures or hazards that cause damage or ill effects. A creature that leaves the area can act normally in the following round. A creature that stays in the area must succeed on another save on its turn or remain affected.",
"Aura of Protection or Destruction": "You radiate otherworldly energy that warps the fate of every creature within 30 feet of you. Decide whether to call on a celestial or a fiend. A celestial fills the area with an aura of nonviolence, and until the start of your next turn every attack roll made by or against a creature inside it counts as a natural 1. A fiend fills it with an aura of violence, and until the start of your next turn every attack roll made by or against a creature inside it, including you, counts as a natural 20.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** The duration increases by 1 round for each slot level above 3rd.",
"Aura of Wrath": "You surround yourself with a psychic aura that drives creatures within 20 feet into violent frenzy. A creature in the aura, or entering it, must succeed on a Wisdom save or be overcome with rage. A raging creature must use its next action to attack the nearest possible target, choosing randomly among equals. It uses a melee weapon if it has one, and otherwise any other offensive ability. If an affected creature leaves the aura, the effect persists for 1 more round. An affected creature repeats the save at the start of each of its turns, ending the effect on itself on a success. A creature that succeeds on a save cannot be affected again by the same aura. If an affected creature hits you with an attack that targets you, it takes 4d8 psychic damage.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** For each slot level above 6th, creatures that leave the aura stay affected for 1 more round, and the damage from striking you increases by 2d8.",
"Auspicious Warning": "Just in time, you call out a lucky warning to a creature within range. As a reaction when an ally makes an attack roll, ability check, or saving throw, the target rolls a d4 and adds the result to the roll it just made, and uses the new total to decide success or failure.",
"Avoid Grievous Injury": "You cast this spell when a foe hits you with a critical hit, before damage dice are rolled. The critical hit against you becomes a normal hit.",
"Avronin's Astral Assembly": "You alert a number of creatures you know, up to your spellcasting ability modifier (minimum 1), that you wish to speak with them through spiritual projection. The invitation can cross any distance and even other planes. Once notified, a creature can accept at any time during the duration. When it does, its spirit is projected into one of the gems used as the casting component, and its body falls unconscious and needs no food or air. Its consciousness is present in the room with you, and its normal form appears as an astral projection within 5 feet of its gem. You can see and hear everyone in the assembly and they can see and hear you and each other as if present, but they cannot physically interact with anything. A creature can end the effect on itself at any time, as can you. When the effect ends or the duration runs out, each spirit returns to its body and the creature wakes. A creature that withdraws cannot rejoin, even if the spell is still active. If a gem is broken while holding an astral self, the spirit returns to its body and the creature gains two levels of exhaustion.",
"Awaken Object": "You spend the casting time enchanting a ruby together with a nonmagical object of humanoid form that is Large or smaller, then touch the ruby to the object. The ruby dissolves into the object, which becomes a living, sentient construct. If it has no face, a humanoid face appears in a fitting spot. It can use an action to make a melee weapon attack against a target within 5 feet. It has free will and acts on its own, speaks one language you know, and is at first friendly to anyone who helped create it. Its speed is 30 feet. If it has no legs or other means of moving, it gains a flying speed of 30 feet and can hover. Its sight and hearing match a typical human's. The GM can raise or lower its Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma to suit unusual cases, such as a statue with high Charisma or a philosopher's bust with high Wisdom. It needs no air, food, water, or sleep. Damage to it can be healed or mechanically repaired. Statistics by size: Tiny: 20 hp, AC 18, 8 to hit, 1d4 + 4 damage. Small: 25 hp, AC 16, 6 to hit, 1d8 + 2 damage. Medium: 40 hp, AC 13, 5 to hit, 2d6 + 1 damage. Large: 50 hp, AC 10, 6 to hit, 2d10 + 2 damage. The source table also lists further values per size: Tiny 4, 18, 10, 2d6, 2d6, 2d6, Small 6, 14, 10, 3d6, 2d6, 2d6, Medium 10, 12, 10, 3d6, 3d6, 2d6, Large 14, 10, 10, 3d6, 3d6, 2d6 + 2, but its column labels are missing.",
"Babble": "You make the speech of affected creatures sound like nonsense. Each creature in a 30-foot-radius sphere centered on a point you choose within range must succeed on an Intelligence save when you cast the spell or be affected. An affected creature cannot communicate in any spoken language it knows, and its words come out as gibberish. It cannot cast spells with verbal components. The spell does not affect telepathy, nonverbal communication, or sounds from creatures with no spoken language. As an action, an affected creature can make another Intelligence save, ending the spell on itself on a success.",
"Bad Timing": "You point at a creature you can see and twist chaotic strands of its fate. If the target fails a Charisma save, the next attack roll or ability check it attempts within 10 minutes is made with disadvantage.",
"Batsense": "For the duration, a creature you touch can make and interpret echolocation squeaks, giving it blindsight out to 60 feet. The target cannot use this blindsight while deafened, and it does not penetrate areas of magical silence. While using it, the target has disadvantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks that depend on being silent. The target also has advantage on Wisdom (Perception) checks that rely on hearing.",
"Battle Mind": "You gain a keen sense of the area around you and can share insights with comrades to give them an edge in combat. You have advantage on Wisdom (Perception) checks made to determine surprise at the start of an encounter, and if you are not surprised, neither are your allies. While in combat, you can use a bonus action on your turn to produce one of these effects (allies must see or hear you to benefit). One ally has advantage on its next attack roll, saving throw, or ability check. Or an enemy has disadvantage on the next attack roll it makes against you or an ally. Or you pinpoint an invisible or hidden creature and tell allies who can see or hear you where it is. This does not remove the creature's advantages, and if it moves, its new position is not shared. Or three allies who can see and hear you gain the benefit of bless, guidance, or resistance on their turns, chosen separately for each. An ally must use the benefit on its turn or lose it.",
"Become Nightwing": "You grow wings of shadow. For the duration you gain a flying speed of 60 feet and a new attack action, Nightwing Breath (Recharge 4 to 6). You exhale shadow in a 30-foot cone. Each creature in the area takes 5d6 necrotic damage, or half with a successful Dexterity save.",
"Bedazzling Charm": "You try to charm a humanoid you can see within range. It makes a Wisdom save, with advantage if you or your companions are fighting or threatening it. On a failure, it is charmed by a third humanoid that you choose and that both of you can see. The spell ends after 1 minute, when you or your companions do anything harmful to either target, or when the third humanoid leaves the charmed creature's sight. The charmed creature treats the third as a friendly acquaintance and follows to keep it in view. When the spell ends, the creature knows you bedazzled it into favoring the third humanoid.",
"Beguiling Bet": "You issue a challenge to one creature you can see within range. It must succeed on a Wisdom save or be charmed. On a failure, you can make an ability check as a bonus action. For example, a Strength (Athletics) check to climb a hard surface or jump high, a Dexterity (Acrobatics) check to do a backflip, or a Charisma (Performance) check to hit a high note or improvise a rhyme. You can use your spellcasting ability modifier in place of the usual one, and you add your proficiency bonus if you are proficient in the skill. The charmed creature must use its next action (which can be a legendary action) to make the same check in a contest against yours. Even if it cannot do the task, such as having no nearby wall to climb or no hands to strum a lute, it must still try as best it can. If you win, the spell and the contest continue, and you make a new check as a bonus action on your turn. The spell ends when it expires or when the creature wins the contest.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** For every two slot levels above 2nd, you can target one more creature. Each creature must be within 30 feet of another when you cast the spell.",
"Benediction": "You call down a blessing in the name of an angel of protection. A creature you can see within range glows with faint white light. The next time it takes damage, it rolls a d4 and reduces the damage by the result. The spell then ends.",
}
